const researchedFiringEditorial:Record<string,{sections:SpecRecord["sections"];faqs:SpecRecord["faqs"]}>={
  "toyota/22r/firing-order":{sections:[
    {heading:"Factory-backed order and the exact installation covered",paragraphs:["The 1990 Toyota Pickup 22R factory-service procedure lists 1-3-4-2 and describes distributor installation with cylinder 1 on its compression stroke. Toyota specification data for the 22R-E confirms the same order, but carbureted 22R and fuel-injected 22R-E timing checks are not identical. Match the emissions label and repair procedure before adjusting base timing.","The four cylinders form one row from the timing-chain end toward the transmission. That makes cylinder identity straightforward, but the distributor tower chosen for number 1 still depends on correct shaft engagement and rotor orientation. Never choose the first tower only because a generic cap drawing puts it at twelve o'clock." ]},
    {heading:"Setting cylinder 1 at compression TDC",paragraphs:["Remove the number 1 plug, rotate the engine in its normal direction and verify compression—not merely alignment of the crank mark. At top dead center on the exhaust stroke, the mark can also align, but the distributor will be one crank revolution out for installation. Toyota's procedure uses valve-train position as a second check before the distributor is seated.","Once the rotor and signal components align as specified, seat the distributor without forcing it. Gear engagement can rotate the shaft as it enters, so compare the final rotor position with the service illustration. Install the cap and route 1-3-4-2 in the established direction one lead at a time." ]},
    {heading:"Troubleshooting after wires or a distributor were disturbed",paragraphs:["An engine that will not start after service often has number 1 indexed on the exhaust stroke, a one-tooth distributor error, or crossed 2 and 3 leads. Recheck mechanical TDC and routing before turning the housing far enough to hide the original problem.","A running engine with poor power or detonation needs the year-specific base-timing procedure. Vacuum-hose handling on a carbureted 22R differs from check-connector terminal handling on a 22R-E. The firing order stays 1-3-4-2; the method used to let the distributor or ECU establish base timing does not." ]},
  ],faqs:[{q:"What is the Toyota 22R firing order?",a:"1-3-4-2 for the factory 22R application covered here; Toyota data also confirms that order for the 22R-E."},{q:"Which end is cylinder 1 on a 22R?",a:"Cylinder 1 is at the front/timing-chain end of the inline engine."},{q:"Why can the timing mark align on the wrong stroke?",a:"The crank reaches TDC twice in a four-stroke cycle. Distributor installation requires number 1 at compression TDC, not exhaust TDC."},{q:"Do the 22R and 22R-E use the same timing-adjustment steps?",a:"No. They share the firing order, but base-timing preparation and controls differ. Follow the exact model-year procedure."}]},
  "jeep/4-0/firing-order":{sections:[
    {heading:"What the 2000 Cherokee manual establishes",paragraphs:["The 2000 Cherokee factory manual identifies the 4.0L as an inline-six, numbers cylinders 1 through 6 from front to rear and gives the mechanical firing order as 1-5-3-6-2-4. That physical numbering remains the foundation for interpreting P030x codes, compression readings and injector circuits.","The 2000 system does not use the earlier distributor-cap routing. Its three coils fire pairs 1-6, 2-5 and 3-4. Each pair receives spark together: one plug fires a cylinder on compression while its paired plug fires on the exhaust stroke. Those pairs describe coil operation, not the order of six power strokes." ]},
    {heading:"Distributor versus coil-pack service",paragraphs:["On earlier 4.0L distributor engines, the cap leads must follow 1-5-3-6-2-4 in the verified rotor direction, and distributor indexing begins with cylinder 1 on compression TDC. On the covered 2000 coil-pack system, moving a distributor is not part of ignition service because there is no cap to clock.","Keep the model year in the diagnostic plan. A picture of an early distributor can teach cylinder numbering but cannot identify the 2000 coil terminals or wiring. Conversely, a 2000 paired-coil diagram should not be used to route the six plug wires around an older cap." ]},
    {heading:"Using cylinder identity during a misfire test",paragraphs:["A P0306 code identifies the rearmost cylinder, number 6; it does not mean the sixth item a technician happened to inspect. Record codes and freeze-frame data, check the plug and lead, then compare spark and compression with a known cylinder under the same conditions.","Because the system is waste-spark, a coil or shared primary-circuit problem may affect both members of a pair. If symptoms involve 1 and 6 together, for example, the 1-6 coil and its control circuit deserve attention. A single-cylinder fault can still come from the plug, lead, injector, vacuum leak or engine mechanics." ]},
  ],faqs:[{q:"What is the Jeep 4.0 firing order?",a:"1-5-3-6-2-4."},{q:"How are Jeep 4.0 cylinders numbered?",a:"They run 1 through 6 from the radiator/front of the engine toward the transmission."},{q:"What are the 2000 Cherokee 4.0 coil pairs?",a:"The waste-spark pairs are 1-6, 2-5 and 3-4. These pairs are not the six-event firing order."},{q:"Can ignition timing be adjusted on a 2000 Cherokee 4.0?",a:"The factory manual states that ignition timing is PCM-controlled and not adjustable."}]},
  "ford/300-inline-6/firing-order":{sections:[
    {heading:"Exact 1996 F-150 4.9L specification",paragraphs:["Ford's factory-service data for the 1996 F-150 L6-300/4.9L lists 1-5-3-6-2-4. The 300-cubic-inch and 4.9-liter names refer to the same familiar truck inline-six family, but the ignition and emissions hardware changed across its long production life.","The cylinders form a single row, numbered from the front accessory/radiator end toward the flywheel. Use that physical row for scan data and compression tests. Use the service illustration and the distributor's actual rotor position—not a memory-based clock-face drawing—to identify the number 1 cap terminal." ]},
    {heading:"A distributor setup that preserves the diagnosis",paragraphs:["Before removing the cap, mark the number 1 lead, photograph the complete routing and mark the rotor and housing positions. If the distributor must come out, establish number 1 compression TDC. Feeling compression or observing both number 1 valves closed distinguishes the compression stroke from the equally possible exhaust-stroke TDC mark.","Seat the distributor fully and account for rotor movement as the helical gear engages. Then route the remaining leads through 1-5-3-6-2-4 in the correct rotation direction for that distributor. Separators matter: allowing adjacent leads to lie against exhaust heat or cross sensitive routing paths can create intermittent ignition faults." ]},
    {heading:"SPOUT, base timing and common false fixes",paragraphs:["On EEC-IV applications such as the covered truck, base-timing work uses the Spark Output (SPOUT) connector so the technician can separate distributor base setting from computer-controlled advance. The under-hood Vehicle Emission Control Information label is the application authority for the exact timing value and preparation.","Do not cure crossed wires by rotating the distributor until the engine barely runs. That trades a visible routing error for incorrect base timing. Restore number 1 compression-TDC indexing, verify all six leads, then perform the applicable base-timing procedure and reconnect the SPOUT circuit." ]},
  ],faqs:[{q:"What is the 1996 Ford F-150 4.9L/300 firing order?",a:"Ford factory-service data lists 1-5-3-6-2-4."},{q:"Where is cylinder 1 on the Ford 300 inline-six?",a:"It is the front cylinder nearest the radiator/accessory end; numbering continues toward the flywheel."},{q:"Are Ford 300 and Ford 4.9L different engines?",a:"Those labels commonly describe the same displacement family, but the model year still controls ignition, emissions and timing procedures."},{q:"What does the SPOUT connector do during timing service?",a:"Disconnecting it isolates the distributor's base setting from PCM-commanded advance on applicable EEC-IV systems. Follow the exact under-hood label and service procedure."}]},
  "chevrolet/250-inline-6/firing-order":{sections:[
    {heading:"What Chevrolet published for the 1971 Turbo-Thrift 250",paragraphs:["Chevrolet's 1971 vehicle information kit lists the Turbo-Thrift 250 as an inline-six, numbers the cylinders 1-2-3-4-5-6 from front to rear and specifies firing order 1-5-3-6-2-4. Keeping the year and engine name beside the order prevents a modern coil-pack diagram or an unrelated V6 bank layout from entering the job.","The mechanical order alone does not establish initial timing, point gap, dwell or emissions-hose routing. Those values can vary with transmission, emissions calibration and distributor identification. Use the vehicle's service data and distributor number for the rest of the tune-up." ]},
    {heading:"Routing a distributor without guessing the number 1 tower",paragraphs:["Photograph and label the existing wires before removal. If the routing is already unknown, bring cylinder 1 to compression TDC by confirming pressure at the front cylinder and aligning the timing reference. Note where the installed rotor points; that corresponding cap tower becomes the number 1 reference only when the distributor is correctly indexed.","Route the remaining leads in the specified distributor direction through 1-5-3-6-2-4. Keep the wires in separators and away from exhaust heat and sharp brackets. A neat route is not cosmetic—it reduces abrasion, heat damage and inductive crossfire between long parallel leads." ]},
    {heading:"Diagnosing a no-start or rough run after tune-up",paragraphs:["A crank-no-start immediately after wire replacement usually calls for a routing audit before new parts. Confirm the front cylinder is number 1, verify compression-stroke TDC, check that every lead reaches the intended cap tower and make sure the coil lead is seated at both ends.","If the engine runs but backfires, do not rotate the distributor as the first response. Crossed leads, one-tooth distributor indexing and compression-versus-exhaust TDC errors can all imitate bad timing. Restore the mechanical reference, route 1-5-3-6-2-4, then set timing with the procedure for the exact 250 application." ]},
  ],faqs:[{q:"What is the 1971 Chevy 250 firing order?",a:"Chevrolet lists 1-5-3-6-2-4."},{q:"How are Chevy 250 cylinders numbered?",a:"They are numbered 1 through 6 from the front/radiator end toward the transmission."},{q:"Is the number 1 distributor tower always at the same clock position?",a:"Do not assume that. The tower corresponds to the rotor position when the distributor is correctly indexed with cylinder 1 at compression TDC."},{q:"Does the firing order also tell me the ignition timing?",a:"No. Firing order and base timing are separate specifications. Use the exact year, calibration and distributor service data for timing."}]},
  "chevrolet/292-inline-6/firing-order":{sections:[
    {heading:"Chevrolet's 1971 292 specification",paragraphs:["Chevrolet's truck information kit identifies the High Torque 292 as an inline, valve-in-head six and lists 1-5-3-6-2-4 for the 250/292 six-cylinder group. The cylinders run 1 through 6 from the front of the engine toward the transmission.","The shared order does not make a 250 and 292 interchangeable. Chevrolet's same table shows different stroke, rod length, output and other specifications. Identify the taller 292 installation and its component numbers before ordering a distributor, manifold, mounts or internal parts." ]},
    {heading:"Finding number 1 without relying on a cap photo",paragraphs:["If the existing wire layout is trustworthy, label every lead and mark the rotor before disturbing it. If it is not, bring the front cylinder to top dead center on its compression stroke. Both valves should be closed and compression should be evident; a timing mark alone can also represent exhaust TDC.","With the distributor correctly engaged, the cap tower above the rotor becomes the number 1 reference. Route the remaining leads 1-5-3-6-2-4 in the verified distributor direction. Preserve separators and clearance from the exhaust manifold." ]},
    {heading:"What firing order cannot tell you",paragraphs:["The sequence cannot select initial timing, point gap, dwell, vacuum advance or governor settings. Chevrolet offered the 292 across truck calibrations, and those surrounding specifications depend on year, emissions package, transmission and distributor identity.","After restoring correct routing, set dwell where applicable before timing because changing point gap changes ignition timing. If the engine still backfires or lacks power, verify compression-TDC indexing and valve timing instead of hiding an error by rotating the distributor." ]},
  ],faqs:[{q:"What is the Chevy 292 inline-six firing order?",a:"The 1971 Chevrolet truck specification lists 1-5-3-6-2-4."},{q:"Where is cylinder 1 on a Chevy 292?",a:"It is the front cylinder nearest the radiator; numbering continues toward the transmission."},{q:"Do Chevy 250 and 292 engines share a firing order?",a:"Yes in the cited 1971 data, but their dimensions, parts and other service specifications are not interchangeable."},{q:"Can a timing-mark check alone prove number 1 is ready for distributor installation?",a:"No. Verify compression TDC because the crank mark can also align at number 1 exhaust TDC."}]},
  "ford/290/firing-order":{sections:[
    {heading:"Why this query must be corrected before a diagram is shown",paragraphs:["Ford Racing's small-block history documents the 289, 302 and 351 families, but not a Ford production 290. AMC did build a 290 V8. A blurred air-cleaner decal, casting transcription or one-digit typing error can therefore change both manufacturer and firing order.","The safe response is identification, not a confident-looking cap diagram. Read the complete casting number and casting date, check the VIN or chassis records, photograph the valve-cover and distributor layout, and note any manufacturer marks before buying wires or moving them." ]},
    {heading:"If the intended engine is a Ford 289",paragraphs:["Ford Racing lists 1-5-4-2-6-3-7-8 for the early 289/302 family. That value is appropriate only after the engine is established as a production-style Ford 289/early 302 with the matching camshaft order.","Even then, do not infer the number 1 cap tower from a generic clock position. Put cylinder 1 at compression TDC, observe the installed rotor and route the cap in the correct rotation direction. Modified engines can use a later 5.0 HO/351W-order camshaft, so camshaft documentation controls a non-stock build." ]},
    {heading:"If the block is an AMC 290 or something else",paragraphs:["Stop using Ford bank diagrams. AMC cylinder numbering, distributor placement and component family must be sourced as AMC information for the exact year. The fact that both are American V8s near the same displacement does not make a Ford order a useful approximation.","When castings conflict with the vehicle badge, treat the engine as swapped until proven otherwise. Record the block, head, intake and distributor identifiers; a mixed or rebuilt engine may need the camshaft maker's firing-order documentation rather than the chassis manual." ]},
  ],faqs:[{q:"Did Ford manufacture a production 290 V8?",a:"The cited Ford Racing engine-family reference documents a 289, not a Ford 290. Verify whether the query is a 289 typo or an AMC 290."},{q:"What is the early Ford 289 firing order?",a:"Ford Racing lists 1-5-4-2-6-3-7-8 for the early 289/302 family."},{q:"Can I use the Ford 289 order on an AMC 290?",a:"No. They are different manufacturers and engine families; identify and source the AMC application separately."},{q:"Can a modified Ford 289 use another firing order?",a:"Yes. A camshaft ground for the later 5.0 HO/351W sequence changes the required order, so the installed camshaft documentation controls."}]},
  "dodge/318/firing-order":{sections:[
    {heading:"Exact 1992 318/5.2L ignition data",paragraphs:["The covered Dodge/Ram service data lists a clockwise distributor and firing order 1-8-4-3-6-5-7-2 for the 318/5.2L SBEC-MPI application. Physical cylinders are odd on the driver/left bank and even on the passenger/right bank, both front to rear.","That application boundary matters because '318' spans carbureted LA engines and electronically managed later versions. The mechanical order is familiar across them, while initial timing, pickup strategy, wiring and emissions controls are not." ]},
    {heading:"Routing the cap after distributor work",paragraphs:["Mark the existing rotor and housing positions before removal. If the crankshaft has moved, establish cylinder 1 compression TDC—not merely a zero timing mark—and install the distributor so the rotor aligns with the intended number 1 tower after the drive engages.","Route 1-8-4-3-6-5-7-2 clockwise on the covered distributor. Move one lead at a time, fully seat every terminal and retain the factory separators. Long parallel wire runs near heat or sharp hardware create faults that can look like a carburetor or injector problem." ]},
    {heading:"Why rotating the distributor is not a universal fix",paragraphs:["The covered 1992 specification says base timing is not adjustable. Applying the older habit of loosening and turning the distributor can disturb synchronization without correcting crossed wires or a mechanical problem.","For an earlier carbureted LA engine, use that exact year's timing procedure. For the 1992 application, restore correct indexing and routing, then diagnose with the applicable electronic-engine-control procedure rather than inventing a base-timing adjustment." ]},
  ],faqs:[{q:"What is the 1992 Dodge 318 firing order?",a:"For the cited 1992 Dodge/Ram 318 application, use 1-8-4-3-6-5-7-2 with clockwise distributor rotation."},{q:"How are Dodge 318 cylinders numbered?",a:"On this 318, the driver/left bank is 1-3-5-7 and the passenger/right bank is 2-4-6-8, front to rear."},{q:"Can I adjust base timing by rotating the distributor on the covered 1992 engine?",a:"No. The cited SBEC-MPI service specification identifies base timing as not adjustable."},{q:"Does this page cover every 318 tune-up procedure?",a:"No. Carbureted LA and later electronic applications require their own timing, wiring and emissions procedures even when the firing order matches."}]},
  "dodge/360/firing-order":{sections:[
    {heading:"Exact 1998 Ram 5.9L/360 layout",paragraphs:["Dodge factory-service data for the 1998 Ram 1500 5.9L identifies the engine as a 360-cubic-inch V8 and lists 1-8-4-3-6-5-7-2. The left/driver bank is 1-3-5-7 and the right/passenger bank is 2-4-6-8, front to rear.","Those physical locations—not firing-event positions—control a P030x diagnosis. Cylinder 6, for example, is the third cylinder back on the passenger bank even though it is the fifth event in the sequence." ]},
    {heading:"Magnum-era distributor service",paragraphs:["A distributor remains part of the system, but the 1998 Magnum's PCM and sensor strategy make the service procedure different from a carbureted LA 360. Photograph the cap, label the wires, mark the housing and rotor, and consult the exact synchronization procedure before rotating anything.","If the distributor has been removed, first prove number 1 compression TDC. Account for rotor movement as the drive engages, then route 1-8-4-3-6-5-7-2 in the verified direction. Correct wire order cannot compensate for a distributor that is indexed incorrectly." ]},
    {heading:"A disciplined misfire check",paragraphs:["Save freeze-frame data before clearing codes. Inspect the affected plug and lead, check distributor cap and rotor condition, and compare compression when the fault stays on one cylinder. Multiple adjacent-cylinder faults may also point to cap tracking, harness issues, intake leaks or mechanical sealing problems.","Do not substitute an older carbureted ignition diagram just because both engines are called 360. The combustion order may match, but connector locations, timing control and diagnostic tests follow the exact model-year system." ]},
  ],faqs:[{q:"What is the 1998 Dodge/Ram 360 firing order?",a:"The cited 1998 Ram 5.9L/360 factory data specifies 1-8-4-3-6-5-7-2."},{q:"How are 5.9L/360 cylinders numbered?",a:"For the covered Magnum, the driver/left bank is 1-3-5-7 and the passenger/right bank is 2-4-6-8, front to rear."},{q:"Is a Dodge 360 the same displacement as the 5.9L Magnum?",a:"The covered factory data identifies the 5.9L V8 as 360 CID, but generation and model year still control parts and procedures."},{q:"Can I use a carbureted LA 360 wiring diagram on a 1998 Magnum?",a:"No. The mechanical order may match, but ignition controls, wiring and distributor service are application-specific."}]},
};

function firingPage(j:FiringJob):SpecRecord{
  const record:SpecRecord={slug:j.slug,keyword:j.keyword,make:j.make,model:j.model,category:"Firing Order",title:j.title,
    metaDescription:`${j.title}: ${j.order}, cylinder numbering, compression-TDC setup, routing cautions and official service-information lookup.`,
    answer:j.order.startsWith("No recognized")?`${j.order}. ${j.numbering}`:`The ${j.model} firing order is ${j.order}. ${j.numbering}`,
    scope:j.scope,detail:j.detail,values:[{label:"Firing order",value:j.order,note:"Confirm engine identity"},{label:"Cylinder numbering",value:j.numbering,note:"Orient from the engine front"}],sources:[j.source],reviewed,featureImage:engineImage,featureOverlay:true,
    diagram:{type:"firing",title:`Interactive ${j.model} firing-order map`,caption:"Select the events in order, then confirm physical cylinder locations in the exact service diagram.",points:j.order.match(/\d/g)??["ID"]},
    intro:[j.scope,j.detail],steps:["Confirm engine identity from the VIN, emissions label and casting information.","Bring cylinder 1 to compression TDC before disturbing distributor routing.","Label and trace one wire, coil or injector circuit at a time, then verify with misfire data."],
    sections:[{heading:`Cylinder identification for ${j.model}`,paragraphs:[j.numbering,j.detail]}],
    faqs:[{q:`What is the ${j.keyword}?`,a:j.order.startsWith("No recognized")?`${j.order}; ${j.numbering}`:`${j.order}. ${j.numbering}`},{q:"Is cylinder number the same as firing-event position?",a:"No. Cylinder identity is a physical location; firing order is the sequence in which those cylinders produce power."},{q:"Should the distributor be moved to fix crossed wires?",a:"Not until compression TDC, rotor position and the correct engine-specific routing have been established."}]};
  const editorial=researchedFiringEditorial[j.slug];
  if(editorial){record.sections.unshift(...editorial.sections);record.faqs=editorial.faqs;record.intro=[record.answer,j.scope,j.detail];}
  return record;
}

const jeepSpark2000=source("2000 Jeep Cherokee 4.0L factory service data — spark plug specification","https://charm.li/Jeep/2000/Cherokee%204WD%20L6-4.0L%20VIN%20S/Repair%20and%20Diagnosis/Specifications/Mechanical%20Specifications/Spark%20Plug/","Archived factory service table listing RC12ECC plugs, a 0.89-mm (0.035-in) gap and a 41-N·m installation torque for this exact application.");
const jeepSpark:SpecRecord={slug:"jeep/4-0/spark-plug-gap",keyword:"jeep 4.0 spark plug gap",make:"Jeep",model:"2000 Cherokee 4.0L",category:"Ignition Specs",title:"2000 Jeep Cherokee 4.0 Spark Plug Gap and Torque",
  metaDescription:"2000 Jeep Cherokee 4.0 spark plug gap, RC12ECC plug specification, installation torque, measurement steps and model-year cautions.",
  answer:"For the 2000 Jeep Cherokee 4.0L, the factory service table specifies a 0.035-in (0.89-mm) spark plug gap, Champion RC12ECC plugs and 41 N·m of installation torque.",
  scope:"These exact values apply to the 2000 Cherokee 4WD 4.0L VIN S application in the cited service data. Other Jeep 4.0 model years can retain the 0.035-in gap while publishing a different plug or tightening value, so do not transfer the whole row by engine nickname alone.",
  detail:"Match plug reach, seat, resistor design and heat range as well as gap. Measure on an undamaged plug, hand-start it into a cool aluminum head, and use the exact year-specific installation procedure; thread coatings alter the torque-to-clamp relationship.",
  values:[{label:"Spark plug gap",value:"0.035 in",note:"0.89 mm"},{label:"Factory plug",value:"Champion RC12ECC",note:"Covered 2000 application"},{label:"Installation torque",value:"41 N·m",note:"Use exact-year service row"},{label:"Engine/application",value:"4.0L VIN S",note:"2000 Cherokee 4WD"}],sources:[jeepSpark2000],reviewed,featureImage:engineImage,featureOverlay:true,
  diagram:{type:"spark",title:"2000 Jeep 4.0 spark-plug inspection guide",caption:"Measure the ground-to-center-electrode gap without striking or levering against the center electrode.",points:["Confirm RC12ECC","Inspect","Measure 0.035 in","Hand-start","Torque"]},intro:["The 0.035-inch number is useful only with an application boundary. This page anchors it to the 2000 Cherokee 4.0L service row rather than implying every AMC/Jeep 4.0 tune-up detail is identical.","A correct gap cannot rescue the wrong plug design, damaged threads or incorrect installation torque."],
  steps:["Confirm the 2000 Cherokee 4.0L VIN S application and approved plug before removal.","Work on a cool engine, remove one coil-rail boot or lead position at a time, and keep debris out of the plug well.","Inspect the new RC12ECC plug and check 0.035 in with a wire gauge; adjust only the ground electrode.","Thread the plug fully by hand to prevent cross-threading, then tighten with a calibrated torque wrench to the exact service value.","Reinstall ignition components and verify smooth operation before clearing any stored misfire information."],
  sections:[{heading:"Why a model-year-specific Jeep 4.0 row matters",paragraphs:["The 4.0L name spans many Cherokee, Wrangler and Grand Cherokee years as ignition hardware evolved from distributors to coil-rail systems. The commonly repeated 0.035-inch gap does not prove that plug part number, torque or removal procedure is identical across them.","For example, earlier factory service data can publish a different tightening value even when gap remains 0.035 in. Use the under-hood label and the service information for the exact year and body before applying more than the gap itself."]},{heading:"Measuring and installing without creating a misfire",paragraphs:["Use a wire-style gauge across the specified electrode surfaces. Reject a plug with cracked porcelain, a bent fine center electrode or damaged threads. If adjustment is permitted, move only the ground strap and remeasure; never hammer a plug against a bench.","Start every plug by hand in a cool head. Resistance before the gasket reaches the seat signals dirt or cross-threading, not a need for more leverage. Unless the exact instructions account for lubricant, keep the threads clean and dry because anti-seize changes clamp load at a given wrench setting."]},{heading:"What to check when a misfire remains",paragraphs:["A correct plug gap is only one branch of diagnosis. Record codes and freeze-frame data, inspect the coil rail or leads and boots for tracking, and compare the removed plugs by cylinder. Oil fouling, coolant deposits or one unusually clean plug can point beyond ignition service.","If the fault stays on the same cylinder after known-good ignition checks, continue with injector, vacuum-leak, compression and leak-down testing. Replacing all plugs repeatedly will not correct a valve, ring, head-gasket or fuel-delivery problem."]}],
  faqs:[{q:"What is the spark plug gap on a 2000 Jeep Cherokee 4.0?",a:"The cited factory service row specifies 0.035 inch, or 0.89 mm."},{q:"Which plug does the 2000 Jeep 4.0 service table list?",a:"Champion RC12ECC for the covered 2000 Cherokee 4.0L VIN S application."},{q:"What is the 2000 Jeep 4.0 spark plug torque?",a:"The cited service table lists 41 N·m. Do not assume that value for every Jeep 4.0 model year."},{q:"Can I use anti-seize on the plug threads?",a:"Only when the exact vehicle or plug instructions explicitly account for it. Lubricated threads can produce excessive clamp load at a dry-thread torque."}]};

